            {
              "id": "02ed1247-fd79-3f90-a997-bd474a60a706",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function is vulnerable to buffer overflow attacks if the size of the data being copied is larger than the size of the destination buffer. In the provided code snippet, if `len` is larger than the size of `paddedVersion`, it can lead to a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that the size of the data being copied does not exceed the size of the destination buffer. This can be done by checking the size of the destination buffer and the size of the data to be copied before calling `memcpy`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t paddedVersionSize = sizeof(paddedVersion);\nif (len <= paddedVersionSize) {\n    memcpy(paddedVersion, apiValue.c_str(), len);\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this fix, we first get the size of `paddedVersion` using `sizeof`. We then check if `len` is less than or equal to `paddedVersionSize` before calling `memcpy`. If `len` is larger than `paddedVersionSize`, we handle the error appropriately.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstring>`: This library provides the `memcpy` function.\n- `<string>`: This library provides the `std::string` class, which is used for `apiValue`.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
