            {
              "id": "cc32fb7e-28be-3bfe-8541-9a98161bbbd3",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen(input)` function in C++ is used to calculate the length of a string. However, it can lead to a vulnerability if the input string is not null-terminated. This is because `strlen()` does not check for null termination and continues reading memory until it encounters a null byte, potentially leading to buffer over-reads, crashes, or information leaks.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that your strings are null-terminated. This can be done by initializing your strings with a null character or by manually adding a null character at the end of your strings. Additionally, consider using safer alternatives to `strlen()`, such as `strnlen()`, which takes a maximum length parameter and stops reading after reaching this length, even if it has not encountered a null byte.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n\nchar input[100];\n// ... populate input ...\nsize_t length = strlen(input);\n```\n\nUse:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n\nchar input[100];\n// ... populate input ...\ninput[sizeof(input) - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null termination\nsize_t length = strlen(input);\n```\n\nOr use `strnlen()`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n\nchar input[100];\n// ... populate input ...\nsize_t length = strnlen(input, sizeof(input));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<cstring>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)"
