            {
              "id": "c4176ab2-458c-3290-a9ca-dfaf1d1654f0",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen()` function in C++ is used to find the length of a string. It returns the number of characters in a string up to but not including the first null character. The vulnerability arises when the string does not have a null character, causing `strlen()` to continue reading until it finds a null character, potentially leading to a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that your strings are null-terminated. This can be done by initializing your strings with a null character or by manually adding a null character at the end of your string. Also, consider using safer alternatives to `strlen()`, such as `strnlen()`, which takes an additional parameter to specify the maximum number of characters to be read.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n\nchar str[] = \"Hello, World!\";\nint len = strlen(str);\n```\n\nUse:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n\nchar str[] = \"Hello, World!\";\nint len = strnlen(str, sizeof(str));\n```\n\nIn the fixed code, `strnlen()` is used instead of `strlen()`. The second parameter to `strnlen()` is the maximum number of characters that `strnlen()` will read. If `str` does not have a null character within this limit, `strnlen()` will stop reading and return the limit.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<cstring>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Buffer_overflow_attack)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "81b4aad6-4666-3fff-bfa0-efbade111e1c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA \"Vulnerable Hash Algorithm\" vulnerability refers to the use of a weak or broken cryptographic hash function. In C++ programming, this vulnerability can occur when a developer uses a hash function that has known vulnerabilities, such as SHA1. SHA1 is considered weak due to known collision vulnerabilities, meaning that two different inputs can produce the same hash output, which can lead to security issues such as data integrity violations and unauthorized information disclosure.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use a stronger, more secure hash function such as SHA-256 or SHA-3. These hash functions are currently considered secure and do not have known collision vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the use of SHA1 with a more secure hash function. For example, if you are using the OpenSSL library, you can replace the use of the `SHA1()` function with the `SHA256()` function.\n\n```cpp\n// Old vulnerable code\nunsigned char* input = reinterpret_cast<unsigned char*>(data);\nunsigned char hash[SHA_DIGEST_LENGTH];\nSHA1(input, strlen(data), hash);\n\n// Recommended fix\nunsigned char* input = reinterpret_cast<unsigned char*>(data);\nunsigned char hash[SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH];\nSHA256(input, strlen(data), hash);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe above code example requires the OpenSSL library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS126858",
                  "81b4aad6-4666-3fff-bfa0-efbade111e1c"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "4e1c96da-b44b-3f71-bea4-f83fedeace78",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA \"Vulnerable Hash Algorithm\" vulnerability refers to the use of weak or broken cryptographic hash functions in a software application. In C++ programming, this vulnerability can occur when a developer uses a hash function that is known to be vulnerable to collision attacks, such as SHA-1.\n\nSHA-1 is a cryptographic hash function that produces a 160-bit (20-byte) hash value. It is commonly used in various security applications and protocols. However, SHA-1 is not considered secure against well-funded attackers. In particular, SHA-1 is vulnerable to collision attacks, where two different inputs produce the same hash output. This can lead to various security issues, such as data integrity violations and unauthorized information disclosure.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, developers should avoid using SHA-1 and instead use stronger hash functions, such as SHA-256 or SHA-3. These hash functions are currently considered secure and are not known to be vulnerable to collision attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf you are using the OpenSSL library to compute SHA-1 hashes, you can replace the `SHA1` function with the `SHA256` function to compute SHA-256 hashes. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <openssl/sha.h>\n\nvoid compute_hash(const unsigned char* data, size_t len, unsigned char* md) {\n    SHA256(data, len, md);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe above code example requires the OpenSSL library. You can install it on Ubuntu with the following command:\n\n```bash\nsudo apt-get install libssl-dev\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS126858"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b5f5cece-45c9-398e-bd32-40a18f7d04f6",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA \"Vulnerable Hash Algorithm\" vulnerability refers to the use of weak or broken cryptographic hash functions in a software application. In C++ programming, this vulnerability can occur when a developer uses a hash function that is known to be vulnerable to collision attacks, such as SHA-1.\n\nSHA-1 is a cryptographic hash function that produces a 160-bit (20-byte) hash value. It is commonly used in various security applications and protocols. However, SHA-1 is not considered secure against well-funded attackers. In particular, SHA-1 is vulnerable to collision attacks, where two different inputs produce the same hash output. This can lead to various security issues, such as data integrity violations and unauthorized information disclosure.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, developers should avoid using SHA-1 and instead use stronger hash functions, such as SHA-256 or SHA-3. These hash functions are currently considered secure and are not known to be vulnerable to collision attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf you are using the OpenSSL library to compute SHA-1 hashes, you can replace the `SHA1` function with the `SHA256` function to compute SHA-256 hashes. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <openssl/sha.h>\n\nvoid compute_hash(const unsigned char* data, size_t len, unsigned char* md) {\n    SHA256(data, len, md);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe above code example requires the OpenSSL library. You can install it on Ubuntu with the following command:\n\n```bash\nsudo apt-get install libssl-dev\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)"
