            {
              "id": "094a2b87-e1ff-3e52-924e-e2ed7987ce3a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA \"Vulnerable Hash Algorithm\" vulnerability refers to the use of a weak or broken cryptographic hash function. In C++ programming, this vulnerability can occur when a developer uses a hash function that has known vulnerabilities, such as SHA1. SHA1 is considered weak due to known collision vulnerabilities, meaning that two different inputs can produce the same hash output, which can lead to security issues such as data integrity violations and unauthorized information disclosure.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use a stronger, more secure hash function such as SHA-256 or SHA-3. These hash functions are currently considered secure and do not have known collision vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the use of SHA1 with a more secure hash function. For example, if you are using the OpenSSL library, you can replace the use of the `SHA1()` function with the `SHA256()` function.\n\n```cpp\n// Old vulnerable code\nunsigned char* input = reinterpret_cast<unsigned char*>(data);\nunsigned char hash[SHA_DIGEST_LENGTH];\nSHA1(input, strlen(data), hash);\n\n// Recommended fix\nunsigned char* input = reinterpret_cast<unsigned char*>(data);\nunsigned char hash[SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH];\nSHA256(input, strlen(data), hash);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe above code example requires the OpenSSL library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS126858",
                  "094a2b87-e1ff-3e52-924e-e2ed7987ce3a"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6067f85a-d4be-3377-b857-5762e6a10924",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA \"Vulnerable Hash Algorithm\" vulnerability refers to the use of a weak or broken cryptographic hash function. In C++ programming, this vulnerability can occur when a developer uses a hash function that has known vulnerabilities, such as SHA1. SHA1 is considered weak due to known collision vulnerabilities, meaning that two different inputs can produce the same hash output, which can lead to security issues such as data integrity violations and unauthorized information disclosure.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use a stronger, more secure hash function such as SHA-256 or SHA-3. These hash functions are currently considered secure and do not have known collision vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the use of SHA1 with a more secure hash function. For example, if you are using the OpenSSL library, you can replace the use of the `SHA1()` function with the `SHA256()` function.\n\n```cpp\n// Old vulnerable code\nunsigned char* input = reinterpret_cast<unsigned char*>(data);\nunsigned char hash[SHA_DIGEST_LENGTH];\nSHA1(input, strlen(data), hash);\n\n// Recommended fix\nunsigned char* input = reinterpret_cast<unsigned char*>(data);\nunsigned char hash[SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH];\nSHA256(input, strlen(data), hash);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe above code example requires the OpenSSL library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS126858",
                  "6067f85a-d4be-3377-b857-5762e6a10924"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "011248b5-61fb-3e9a-ac8e-71a9be85fca0",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues that can lead to arbitrary code execution or denial of service. The `rewind` function is one such function that can potentially lead to these issues.\n\nThe `rewind` function sets the file position to the beginning of the file for the stream pointed to by stream. It's equivalent to `(void) fseek(stream, 0L, SEEK_SET)`, but `rewind` also clears the error indicator.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using the `rewind` function if possible. Instead, use safer alternatives that perform bounds checking or that limit the potential for buffer overflows and other issues. For example, you can use the `fseek` function with appropriate error checking.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `rewind`, you can use `fseek` as follows:\n\n```cpp\nFILE *fp = fopen(\"file.txt\", \"r\");\nif (fp == NULL) {\n    // handle error\n}\n\n// Instead of rewind(fp);\nif (fseek(fp, 0L, SEEK_SET) != 0) {\n    // handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `rewind` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "43b2a476-391f-3e90-876e-5202d5a10a85",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strncpy` function in C++ is a common source of vulnerabilities due to its potential to cause buffer overflow. This can occur when the size of the destination buffer is less than the length of the source string. If the source string is not null-terminated or if the destination buffer is not large enough to hold the source string plus the null-terminator, this can lead to undefined behavior, including memory corruption, crashes, and security vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer string handling functions that ensure null-termination and prevent buffer overflow. In C++, this can be achieved by using the `std::string` class or the `strlcpy` function if available. Always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the source string plus the null-terminator.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strncpy` function with a safer alternative. For example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string>\n\nstd::string buf;\nstd::string str;\n\nbuf = str.substr(0, errLen);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<string>`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
