              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues that can lead to arbitrary code execution or denial of service. In this case, the `qsort` function is not inherently unsafe, but it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used correctly.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library. In this case, the `std::sort` function can be used as a safer alternative to `qsort`. The `std::sort` function is type-safe and does not require a function pointer, reducing the risk of memory corruption.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `qsort` function with the `std::sort` function. Here is how you can do it:\n\n```cpp\n#include <algorithm> // for std::sort\n\n// ...\n\nstd::sort(lemp->sorted.begin() + 1, lemp->sorted.begin() + lemp->nstate, stateResortCompare);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<algorithm>`: for `std::sort`\n- `<vector>` or `<array>`: if `lemp->sorted` is a container from the C++ Standard Library\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
