                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards and should be avoided in favor of safer alternatives.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, developers should avoid using dangerous C functions and instead use safer alternatives that are less prone to misuse. For example, instead of using `strcpy()`, which can cause buffer overflows if not used carefully, developers can use `strncpy()`, which allows for the specification of a maximum number of characters to copy and can help prevent buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code snippet `j==0` does not contain any prohibited C functions, so no specific fix can be recommended for it.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet `j==0` does not require any library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
