              "id": "dc014ec2-1849-34b8-8f57-fa078f5133d2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards. The use of these functions can lead to serious security vulnerabilities including code execution, privilege escalation, or denial of service.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to avoid using dangerous C functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library or other libraries that perform bounds checking and other safety measures. Always validate and sanitize input data before processing it. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe specific vulnerability sink `j>0` does not seem to be related to a \"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability. It's a simple comparison operation in C++. If there's a specific C function being used in your code that's causing this vulnerability, you should replace it with a safer alternative. For example, if you're using `strcpy()`, consider using `strncpy()` with a size parameter instead.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nWithout a specific code example, it's hard to list the exact library dependencies. However, in general, C++ programs may require the C++ Standard Library (`<iostream>`, `<string>`, etc.) and the C Standard Library (`<stdio.h>`, `<string.h>`, etc.).\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Proactive Controls](https://owasp.org/www-project-proactive-controls/)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
