              }
            },
            {
              "id": "bcedc492-234a-3ace-9c6f-ed5170f06f95",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In this case, the `fprintf` function is used, which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if the format string is not controlled by the programmer.\n\nThe `fprintf` function writes formatted output to the stream pointed to by `out`, under control of the string pointed to by `incName`. If an attacker can control the `incName` string, they can exploit the format string vulnerability to execute arbitrary code, crash the program, or leak information.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using C library functions that do not check the lengths of their input parameters. Instead, use safer alternatives that include length checks. In this case, you can use `snprintf` instead of `fprintf`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer version of the code:\n\n```cpp\nchar buffer[256];\nsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), \"#include \\\"%s\\\"\\n\", incName);\nfputs(buffer, out);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `stdio.h` for `fprintf`, `snprintf`, and `fputs` functions.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-134: Use of Externally-Controlled Format String](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/134.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2209c880-e236-3861-b07c-a618104fe009",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the filename (`tpltname`) is manipulated by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using insecure C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library. Validate all input data to ensure it meets the expected format and size. Avoid using user-supplied data directly in function calls that interact with the system or file system.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `fopen` function with a safer alternative from the C++ Standard Library, such as `std::ifstream`. Here is an example of how to do it:\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n\nstd::ifstream in(tpltname, std::ios::binary);\nif (!in) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `cstdio` for `fopen`\n\nThe fixed code requires the following library:\n\n- `fstream` for `std::ifstream`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "8061092a-84a0-3894-88f4-cf0bc5be7e84",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflow, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflow. In this case, the `fprintf` function is used, which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if not used properly.\n\nFormat string vulnerabilities occur when the submitted data of an input string is evaluated as a command by the application. In this case, the application is vulnerable because it directly uses user input in the `fprintf` function. This can allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code, read the stack, or cause a segmentation fault in the application, leading to a denial of service.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using `fprintf` and similar functions that do not perform format string checks. Instead, use safer alternatives that do not have these issues. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `fprintf` function with `fputs` function:\n\n```cpp\nfputs(\"\\\"\\n\", out);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Code Review Guide](https://owasp.org/www-project-code-review-guide/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n- [CWE-134: Use of Externally-Controlled Format String](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/134.html)"
              },
