            {
              "id": "8ee09e20-b40b-3b8d-b906-4b4dc83f831f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Found\" is a vulnerability that arises when a program uses a function that is considered unsafe due to its potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In this case, the `fprintf` function is used, which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if the format string contains user-supplied data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using functions that do not perform bounds checking or format string checking. Instead, use safer alternatives that include these checks. In the case of `fprintf`, a safer alternative would be `snprintf` or `fprintf_s` (in C11 or later), which includes bounds checking.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `fprintf` function with `snprintf` or `fprintf_s`:\n\n```cpp\nchar buffer[256];\nsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), \"#line %d \\\"\", lineno);\nfputs(buffer, out);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `stdio.h`: for `fprintf`, `snprintf`, and `fputs`.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
