            {
              "id": "b2f20c41-6bfe-3974-9580-f4491f7546da",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Found\" is a vulnerability that occurs when a C++ program uses a function that is considered unsafe. These functions are often prohibited because they do not perform bounds checking or other necessary validation, which can lead to buffer overflow attacks, memory corruption, and other serious security issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, developers should avoid using prohibited C functions in their code. Instead, they should use safer alternatives that perform necessary validation and bounds checking. For example, instead of using `strcpy()`, which does not check the size of the destination buffer, developers should use `strncpy()`, which does.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf the prohibited function is `strcpy()`, it can be replaced with `strncpy()`. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\n// Unsafe code\nchar dest[10];\nstrcpy(dest, src);\n\n// Safe code\nchar dest[10];\nstrncpy(dest, src, sizeof(dest) - 1);\ndest[sizeof(dest) - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nIn the safe code, `strncpy()` is used instead of `strcpy()`. The size of the destination buffer is passed to `strncpy()` to prevent buffer overflow. The last line ensures that the string is null-terminated.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example does not have any library dependencies.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "07317c8d-6851-34e8-bd3a-aa6f5321d383",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string up to but not including the first null character. If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null character, which can lead to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other undefined behavior.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `w = (int)strlen(sp->name)`, the vulnerability arises if `sp->name` is not null-terminated. This can lead to reading of unintended memory, causing undefined behavior or potential security issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that your strings are null-terminated. This can be done by initializing your strings with a null character at the end, or by manually adding a null character at the end of your strings after they have been modified.\n\nAdditionally, consider using safer alternatives to `strlen` such as `strnlen`, which takes an additional parameter specifying the maximum number of characters to read. This can prevent buffer overflows by ensuring that `strnlen` does not read past the end of your buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nif (sp->name != NULL) {\n    w = (int)strnlen(sp->name, MAX_LENGTH);\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `MAX_LENGTH` should be replaced with the maximum expected length of `sp->name`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet requires the `cstring` library for the `strlen` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "126d9205-c07c-3a09-a29f-676d58294a8d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of insecure or unsafe C library functions. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflow, etc. The specific vulnerability sink `sp->prec>=0` does not directly relate to this vulnerability, but it could lead to a null pointer dereference if `sp` is not properly checked for null before this operation.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. Avoid using unsafe C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library.\n2. Always validate and sanitize input data.\n3. Implement proper error handling and exception handling mechanisms.\n4. Always check pointers for null before dereferencing them.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the specific vulnerability sink `sp->prec>=0`:\n\n```cpp\nif (sp != nullptr) {\n    if (sp->prec >= 0) {\n        // rest of the code\n    }\n}\n```\n\nIn this fix, we first check if `sp` is not null before dereferencing it. This prevents a potential null pointer dereference.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example does not provide enough context to determine the specific library dependencies. However, any C++ code generally requires the C++ Standard Library.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-628: Function Call with Incorrectly Specified Arguments](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/628.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "35fcd1bb-a0ce-391d-a241-e443412810d3",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure or unsafe C library functions in the code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In the provided sink `PrintAction(ap,fp,30)`, it's not clear which specific prohibited C function is being used without more context. However, the vulnerability could be related to improper handling of buffers or format strings.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it's recommended to use safer alternatives to the prohibited C functions. For instance, instead of using `strcpy`, use `strncpy` which allows you to specify the maximum number of characters to be copied, thus preventing buffer overflow. Similarly, instead of `sprintf`, use `snprintf`. Always ensure that your buffers are large enough to hold all the data you're putting in them, including the null terminator.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nWithout the specific context of the `PrintAction` function, it's hard to provide a specific fix. However, if it's using a prohibited C function, it should be replaced with a safer alternative. For instance, if `PrintAction` uses `strcpy`, it could be replaced as follows:\n\n```cpp\nvoid PrintAction(char* ap, FILE* fp, size_t size) {\n    char buffer[32];\n    if (size < sizeof(buffer)) {\n        strncpy(buffer, ap, size);\n        buffer[size - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n    }\n    // Rest of the function...\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nWithout more context, it's hard to list all library dependencies required by the code example. However, if `PrintAction` uses file operations or string operations, it might require:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links were active and accessible at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
