                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Identified\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards because safer alternatives exist that are less likely to result in vulnerabilities.\n\nIn the given code snippet, `i==iCursor`, there is no prohibited C function identified. This is a simple comparison operation between two variables `i` and `iCursor`. If there is a prohibited C function in your code, it might be functions like `strcpy()`, `sprintf()`, `gets()`, etc.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should replace the prohibited C functions with safer alternatives. For example, instead of using `strcpy()`, you can use `strncpy()` which allows you to specify the maximum number of characters to be copied, thus preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nAs mentioned, the provided code snippet `i==iCursor` does not contain any prohibited C function. However, if there is a prohibited C function in your code, here is an example of how you can fix it:\n\n```cpp\n// Vulnerable code\nchar dest[50];\nchar src[50];\nstrcpy(dest, src);\n\n// Fixed code\nchar dest[50];\nchar src[50];\nstrncpy(dest, src, sizeof(dest) - 1);\n```\n\nIn the fixed code, `strncpy()` is used instead of `strcpy()`, and the maximum number of characters to be copied is specified as `sizeof(dest) - 1` to prevent buffer overflow.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet does not require any library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
